= Solution
{parent=a}

Use <Minkowski metric> $g_{\mu\nu}=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and units with the <speed of light> equal to one. Thus $u^\mu u_\mu=-1$, and $h_{\mu\nu}=g_{\mu\nu}+u_\mu u_\nu$ is the <spatial projector of a timelike congruence>. Keep the medium velocity fixed when varying the <electromagnetic four-potential>. Since the <electromagnetic field tensor> is $F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu$, its variation gives
$$
\delta_A S=\int d^4x\,\frac12(M^{\mu\nu}-F^{\mu\nu})(\partial_\mu\delta A_\nu-\partial_\nu\delta A_\mu)
=\int d^4x\,(M^{\mu\nu}-F^{\mu\nu})\partial_\mu\delta A_\nu.
$$
Both <tensors> are antisymmetric. <Integration by parts>, with variations vanishing at the boundary, therefore gives
$$
\delta_A S=\int d^4x\,\partial_\mu(F^{\mu\nu}-M^{\mu\nu})\delta A_\nu.
$$
The <Euler-Lagrange field equation> is $\partial_\mu(F^{\mu\nu}-M^{\mu\nu})=0$. Renaming the free index and using antisymmetry gives
$$
\boxed{\partial_\nu F^{\mu\nu}=\partial_\nu M^{\mu\nu}.}
$$
These are the inhomogeneous <Maxwell equations> with the <bound charge and bound current> encoded by the <polarization-magnetization tensor>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

There is a sign inconsistency in the original PDF: varying its action gives a plus sign for the spatially projected magnetic term, whereas its displayed target equation has a minus sign. The following <Lagrange multiplier for antisymmetry> calculation derives the result of the action as printed.

Temporarily vary all sixteen entries $M^{\mu\nu}$ independently and add
$$
\mathcal L_{\lambda}=\frac12\lambda_{\mu\nu}(M^{\mu\nu}+M^{\nu\mu}),\qquad \lambda_{\mu\nu}=\lambda_{\nu\mu}.
$$
Variation of the symmetric <Lagrange multiplier> imposes $M^{\mu\nu}+M^{\nu\mu}=0$. Define
$$
w_\mu=u_\rho M^\rho{}_{\mu},\qquad C_{\mu\nu}=h_{\mu\rho}h_{\nu\sigma}M^{\rho\sigma}.
$$
On this constraint surface, $C_{\nu\mu}=-C_{\mu\nu}$. Differentiating the original contractions before imposing the constraint gives
$$
\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial M^{\mu\nu}}(u_\alpha u_\beta M^{\lambda\alpha}M^\beta{}_{\lambda})\right|_{M=-M^{\mathsf T}}
=u_\nu w_\mu-u_\mu w_\nu,
$$
and
$$
\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial M^{\mu\nu}}(h_{\alpha\rho}h_{\beta\sigma}M^{\alpha\beta}M^{\sigma\rho})\right|_{M=-M^{\mathsf T}}
=-2C_{\mu\nu}.
$$
In particular, the reversed order $\sigma\rho$ in the last factor of the action matters. The unrestricted <Euler-Lagrange field equation>, evaluated on the antisymmetry constraint, is
$$
0=\frac12F_{\mu\nu}-\frac1{2\kappa}(u_\nu w_\mu-u_\mu w_\nu)+\frac1{2\chi}C_{\mu\nu}+\lambda_{\mu\nu}.
$$
Subtract its index-transposed equation to eliminate the symmetric multiplier:
$$
\boxed{0=F_{\mu\nu}-\frac1\kappa\left(u_\nu u_\rho M^\rho{}_{\mu}-u_\mu u_\rho M^\rho{}_{\nu}\right)+\frac1\chi h_{\mu\rho}h_{\nu\sigma}M^{\rho\sigma}.}
$$
A direct check is to restrict the <action> to a rest-frame magnetic component $M^{12}=m$, $M^{21}=-m$: its quadratic contribution is $+m^2/(2\chi)$, whose derivative has a positive sign. To obtain the PDF's target equation instead, one must reverse the sign of that quadratic term in the action, or replace its last $M^{\sigma\rho}$ by $M^{\rho\sigma}$. These are alternative repairs; changing conventions cannot make the printed action and printed equation agree.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Choose $A^\mu=(\Phi,\mathbf A)$, so that $F_{i0}=E_i$ and $F_{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, with $\epsilon_{123}=1$. The definitions of the <polarization-magnetization tensor> imply $M^{0i}=-P_i$ and $M^{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}M_k$. Separating temporal and spatial indices,
$$
\frac12M^{\mu\nu}F_{\mu\nu}=M^{i0}F_{i0}+\frac12M^{ij}F_{ij}
=\mathbf P\cdot\mathbf E+\frac12\epsilon_{ijk}\epsilon_{ij\ell}M_kB_\ell.
$$
The <Levi-Civita symbol> contraction equals $2\delta_{k\ell}$, giving
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L_{\rm int}=\mathbf P\cdot\mathbf E+\mathbf M\cdot\mathbf B.}
$$
Thus $\mathbf P$ is the <electric dipole moment> per volume, or <electric polarization>, and $\mathbf M$ is the <magnetic dipole moment> per volume, or <magnetization>. The signs are those of dipole interactions in a <Lagrangian>; the associated fixed-dipole potential energies have the opposite signs. Consistently, the equation in part (a) has components
$$
\nabla\cdot(\mathbf E+\mathbf P)=0,\qquad
\nabla\times(\mathbf B-\mathbf M)=\partial_t(\mathbf E+\mathbf P),
$$
so $\rho_b=-\nabla\cdot\mathbf P$ and $\mathbf J_b=\partial_t\mathbf P+\nabla\times\mathbf M$ are the <bound charge and bound current>.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

In the rest frame $u^\mu=(1,0,0,0)$ and $h_{\mu\nu}=\operatorname{diag}(0,1,1,1)$. The two material contractions in the printed action become $-\mathbf P^2$ and $-2\mathbf M^2$. Consequently its material <Lagrangian density> is
$$
\mathcal L_{\rm mat}=\mathbf P\cdot\mathbf E+\mathbf M\cdot\mathbf B+\frac{\mathbf P^2}{2\kappa}+\frac{\mathbf M^2}{2\chi}.
$$
The <Euler-Lagrange field equations> are algebraic, since no derivatives of the material variables occur. They give
$$
\boxed{\mathbf P=-\kappa\mathbf E,\qquad \mathbf M=-\chi\mathbf B\quad\text{for the printed action}.}
$$
These also follow from the $i0$ and $ij$ components of the corrected equation in part (b). If instead the intended starting point was the PDF's displayed equation in part (b), its predictions would be
$$
\boxed{\mathbf P=-\kappa\mathbf E,\qquad \mathbf M=+\chi\mathbf B\quad\text{for the printed target equation}.}
$$
The two magnetic answers express the documented sign error, rather than different choices of frame. Here the inverse coefficients in the action require nonzero $\kappa,\chi$; zero response is obtained as a limiting constitutive law.

= e
{parent=1}
