= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Under $\psi\mapsto e^{i\alpha}\psi$ with constant real $\alpha$, the field and its <complex conjugate> acquire opposite phases. These cancel in $|\psi|^2$, $|\psi|^4$, $\nabla\psi^*\cdot\nabla\psi$, and $\psi^*\dot\psi-\psi\dot\psi^*$. The <Lagrangian density> therefore has the <particle-number phase symmetry>, a global <circle group> $U(1)$.

For a spacetime-dependent real $\alpha(\mathbf r,t)$,
$$
\dot\psi'=e^{i\alpha}(\dot\psi+i\dot\alpha\psi),\qquad
\nabla\psi'=e^{i\alpha}(\nabla\psi+i\psi\nabla\alpha).
$$
Writing $n=|\psi|^2$, the change in the <Lagrangian density> is
$$
\Delta\mathcal L=-\hbar n\dot\alpha-\frac{\hbar^2}{2M}\left[
i(\psi\nabla\psi^*-\psi^*\nabla\psi)\cdot\nabla\alpha+n|\nabla\alpha|^2\right].
$$
This is not generally zero or a <boundary term>. Thus **the symmetry is global, with no arbitrary local phase symmetry**. A compensating <gauge field> and <gauge-covariant derivative of a charged scalar field> would be required to promote it to a local redundancy.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Treat $\psi,\psi^*$ independently in the <Legendre transform in mechanics>. Their <canonical momenta> are
$$
\pi_\psi=\frac{i\hbar}{2}\psi^*,\qquad \pi_{\psi^*}=-\frac{i\hbar}{2}\psi.
$$
The time-derivative terms cancel in $\pi_\psi\dot\psi+\pi_{\psi^*}\dot\psi^*-\mathcal L$, giving the <Hamiltonian density>
$$
\boxed{\mathcal H=\frac{\hbar^2}{2M}|\nabla\psi|^2-\mu|\psi|^2+\frac g2|\psi|^4.}
$$
The first-order time dependence makes the momenta constrained; this direct calculation still gives the canonical density. In physical terms this is the <grand-canonical Hamiltonian of a Bose field>, $K=H-\mu N$. The physical energy density is obtained by adding $\mu|\psi|^2$ back. A stationary field in this chemical-potential frame has the usual factor $e^{-i\mu t/\hbar}$ in the physical frame.

Complete the square:
$$
\mathcal H=\frac{\hbar^2}{2M}|\nabla\psi|^2+\frac g2\left(|\psi|^2-\frac\mu g\right)^2-\frac{\mu^2}{2g}.
$$
For uniform or periodic boundary conditions, or in the homogeneous bulk, both nonconstant terms are nonnegative and vanish simultaneously at
$$
\boxed{\psi_0=\sqrt{\frac\mu g}\,e^{i\theta_0},\qquad \mathcal H_{\min}=-\frac{\mu^2}{2g}.}
$$
The <ground states> form a circle. A particular nonzero $\psi_0$ is changed by every nontrivial phase rotation, although the full family is invariant. Selecting one such <mean-field theory> state therefore exhibits <spontaneous symmetry breaking> of $U(1)$. This describes phase selection in the thermodynamic limit; an exact finite-system particle-number <eigenstate> need not itself have a nonzero field expectation.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Choose the real representative $v=\psi_0=\sqrt{\mu/g}$. For $\psi=v+\chi$,
$$
|\psi|^2=v^2+v(\chi+\chi^*)+|\chi|^2,
$$
and the fourth power through quadratic order is
$$
|\psi|^4=v^4+2v^3(\chi+\chi^*)+2v^2|\chi|^2+v^2(\chi+\chi^*)^2+O(\chi^3).
$$
In $\mu|\psi|^2-g|\psi|^4/2$, the linear terms and the $|\chi|^2$ terms cancel because $gv^2=\mu$. The remaining potential contribution is $\mu^2/(2g)-\mu(\chi+\chi^*)^2/2$.

The time-derivative contribution is
$$
\frac{i\hbar}{2}(\psi^*\dot\psi-\psi\dot\psi^*)
=\frac{i\hbar v}{2}\partial_t(\chi-\chi^*)+\frac{i\hbar}{2}(\chi^*\dot\chi-\chi\dot\chi^*).
$$
Its first term is a <total derivative> and contributes only a temporal <boundary term> for fixed endpoint fields. Since $v$ is uniform, the gradient contribution is simply $-\hbar^2|\nabla\chi|^2/(2M)$. Dropping that boundary term and the constant, the <quadratic condensate fluctuation Lagrangian> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L_2=\frac{i\hbar}{2}(\chi^*\dot\chi-\chi\dot\chi^*)-\frac{\hbar^2}{2M}\nabla\chi^*\cdot\nabla\chi-\frac\mu2(\chi+\chi^*)^2.}
$$
The omitted cubic and quartic interactions describe scattering between these small fluctuations; they do not enter the linear <normal mode> calculation.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For independent $\chi,\chi^*$, the derivatives needed in the $\chi^*$ <Euler-Lagrange field equation> are
$$
\frac{\partial\mathcal L_2}{\partial\chi^*}=\frac{i\hbar}{2}\dot\chi-\mu(\chi+\chi^*),\qquad
\frac{\partial\mathcal L_2}{\partial\dot\chi^*}=-\frac{i\hbar}{2}\chi,\qquad
\frac{\partial\mathcal L_2}{\partial(\nabla\chi^*)}=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2M}\nabla\chi.
$$
Consequently the two conjugate field equations are
$$
\boxed{i\hbar\dot\chi=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2M}\nabla^2\chi+\mu(\chi+\chi^*),\qquad
-i\hbar\dot\chi^*=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2M}\nabla^2\chi^*+\mu(\chi+\chi^*).}
$$
Write $\chi=\chi_1+i\chi_2$ with real components, and put $A=\hbar^2/(2M)$. Separating real and imaginary parts gives
$$
-\hbar\dot\chi_2=(-A\nabla^2+2\mu)\chi_1,\qquad
\hbar\dot\chi_1=-A\nabla^2\chi_2.
$$
For each real field take the real part of a complex <plane wave> amplitude, $\chi_j=\operatorname{Re}[q_j e^{i(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)}]$. With $\epsilon_k=\hbar^2|\mathbf k|^2/(2M)$ the two equations become
$$
i\hbar\omega q_2=(\epsilon_k+2\mu)q_1,\qquad
-i\hbar\omega q_1=\epsilon_k q_2.
$$
A nonzero <normal mode> requires their <determinant> to vanish, so
$$
\hbar^2\omega^2=\epsilon_k(\epsilon_k+2\mu).
$$
The positive-frequency <Bogoliubov quasiparticle dispersion> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\omega(\mathbf k)=\frac1\hbar\sqrt{\epsilon_k(\epsilon_k+2\mu)}
=\sqrt{\frac{|\mathbf k|^2}{2M}\left(\frac{\hbar^2|\mathbf k|^2}{2M}+2\mu\right)}.}
$$
There is also the negative-frequency conjugate solution, as required for real component fields. Since $\mu>0$, $\omega^2\geq0$, consistent with the stability of the chosen uniform <ground state>.

= e
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

At long wavelength,
$$
\boxed{\omega(k)=c_s k+O(k^3),\qquad c_s=\sqrt{\frac\mu M},\qquad \omega(0)=0.}
$$
This is the sound-like <Goldstone mode> of the broken global $U(1)$ <continuous symmetry>. A spatially uniform infinitesimal phase change is $\delta\psi=i\psi_0\delta\theta$, entirely in the $\chi_2$ direction, and costs no potential energy. Slow spatial variations acquire gradient energy and propagate as sound. Thus the <dispersion relation> realizes <Goldstone's theorem> through a gapless excitation; it is not a massive phase oscillation.

The amplitude and phase are canonically paired by the first-order time derivative in the <quadratic condensate fluctuation Lagrangian>. There is one positive-frequency branch, rather than an additional independent amplitude branch of a relativistic second-order-time theory. At high momentum,
$$
\hbar\omega=\epsilon_k+\mu+O(\mu^2/\epsilon_k),
$$
so the same branch becomes particle-like. The <healing length> $\xi=\hbar/\sqrt{2M\mu}$ sets the crossover scale.

\Image[2019-q3-condensate-dispersion.png]
{title=Gapless Bogoliubov dispersion and its sound and particle limits}
{description=Original dimensionless curves with $q=k\xi$ and $E/\mu=\hbar\omega/\mu$. The exact curve is $q\sqrt{q^2+2}$, the sound asymptote is $\sqrt2q$, and the large-momentum asymptote is $q^2+1$.}

= 4
{parent=2019}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <Landau free energy> is a coarse-grained thermodynamic potential expressed as a function of a proposed macroscopic <order parameter>. Its coefficients include microscopic and thermal effects already averaged over. At fixed external thermodynamic conditions, equilibrium selects its <global minimum>; other <local minima> can represent <metastable> states. It is not the internal energy of a single microscopic magnetic configuration. Here the real scalar $m$ represents a selected component of <magnetization>: zero describes the disordered state, while nonzero values distinguish two oppositely ordered states.

Every power is even, so the internal <discrete symmetry> is
$$
\boxed{m\longmapsto-m,\qquad \mathbb Z_2.}
$$
For a magnetic <order parameter>, this represents reversal of the magnetization, as under <time reversal> in zero applied <magnetic field>. A nonzero field would generally add an odd term and break this symmetry explicitly. This scalar model has no continuous internal rotation symmetry; physical rotational symmetry of a vector magnet is not implied by treating one real component. The condition $c>0$ makes the polynomial bounded below at large $|m|$.

For the requested ordered phase on cooling, take the usual implicit assumption $a>0$, and write $r=a(T-T_c)$. If $a<0$ the temperature directions reverse; if $a=0$ this expansion has no temperature-driven transition. The source explicitly states only $c>0$.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The stationary points of the <Landau free energy> obey
$$
f'(m)=2m(r+bm^2+cm^4)=0.
$$
For $b>0$, $r\geq0$ makes every term of $f$ nonnegative, so $m=0$ is the unique <global minimum>. For $r<0$, its curvature $f''(0)=2r$ is negative. Setting $x=m^2$ gives $cx^2+bx+r=0$, whose unique positive root is
$$
x_+=\frac{-b+\sqrt{b^2-4cr}}{2c}.
$$
At a nonzero stationary point,
$$
f''(m)=4x(b+2cx),\qquad f(m)=-\frac b2x^2-\frac{2c}{3}x^3.
$$
Thus both $m=\pm\sqrt{x_+}$ are stable and have lower <free energy> than $m=0$. The <equilibrium magnetization> is
$$
\boxed{m_{\rm eq}=\begin{cases}
0,&T\geq T_c,\\
\displaystyle\pm\left[\frac{-b+\sqrt{b^2+4ac(T_c-T)}}{2c}\right]^{1/2},&T<T_c.
\end{cases}}
$$
For fixed $b>0$ and $T\to T_c^-$, expand the square root:
$$
m_{\rm eq}^2=\frac{a(T_c-T)}b+O((T_c-T)^2).
$$
It tends continuously to zero. Hence the <continuous phase transition> has the <order-parameter critical exponent>
$$
\boxed{|m_{\rm eq}|\sim\sqrt{\frac ab}(T_c-T)^{1/2},\qquad\beta=\frac12.}
$$
This is the <mean-field critical exponent>; fluctuation corrections beyond <Landau theory> are not included.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $b<0$ at $T=T_c$, the quartic term lowers the <free energy> near $m=0$, while the positive sextic term stabilizes it at larger $|m|$. The nonzero stationary points have
$$
\boxed{m^2=-\frac bc,\qquad f(m)=\frac{b^3}{6c^2}<0.}
$$
They are <global minima>. The stationary point $m=0$ has zero quadratic curvature but is a flat local maximum because its leading nonzero term is negative. Thus the system is already ordered at $T_c$, with a finite <magnetization>.

More explicitly, a continuous onset from $m=0$ could occur only where $f''(0)=2r$ vanishes, namely at $r=0$. But the global minima there are the finite values above. For sufficiently large positive $r$, only $m=0$ is stable. Therefore the equilibrium minimum must change at some positive $r$ by crossing between a zero and a finite nonzero <order parameter>. This is a <first-order phase transition>. Its actual temperature lies above $T_c$ and is calculated next; $T_c$ itself is the disordered <spinodal point>, not the coexistence temperature.

\Image[2019-q4-landau-phase-diagram.png]
{title=Free-energy coexistence and the continuous and first-order Landau transition lines}
{description=Original plots. The left panel uses $b=-1,c=1$ at $r=0$, at coexistence $r=3/16$, and above coexistence $r=0.24$. The right panel uses $a=c=1$ and shows the equilibrium phase boundaries in the $(T-T_c,b)$ plane. Dashed gray curves are spinodal limits, not additional equilibrium transitions.}

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

At <phase coexistence>, the finite ordered <global minimum> has the same <free energy> as $m=0$. With $x=m^2>0$ and $f(0)=0$, stationarity and equal values give
$$
r+bx+cx^2=0,\qquad r+\frac b2x+\frac c3x^2=0.
$$
Subtracting eliminates $r$:
$$
\frac b2x+\frac{2c}{3}x^2=0,\qquad x_*=-\frac{3b}{4c}>0.
$$
Substitute back to get $r_*=3b^2/(16c)$, so
$$
\boxed{T_c^*=T_c+\frac{3b^2}{16ac},\qquad
m_{\rm jump}=\sqrt{-\frac{3b}{4c}}\quad(b<0).}
$$
The three degenerate equilibrium values at this temperature are $0$ and $\pm m_{\rm jump}$. A useful global check, avoiding confusion with a <spinodal point>, is the exact factorization
$$
\left.f(m)\right|_{r=r_*}=\frac c3m^2\left(m^2+\frac{3b}{4c}\right)^2\geq0.
$$
All three zeros are therefore <global minima>. Their curvatures are positive: $f''(0)=3b^2/(8c)$ and $f''(\pm m_{\rm jump})=3b^2/(2c)$.

The ordered local minimum first appears on cooling at $r=b^2/(4c)$, where the nonzero stationary roots merge. It becomes globally stable only at $r_*$; the disordered minimum remains <metastable> down to $r=0$. These different temperatures distinguish <phase coexistence> from loss of local stability.

= e
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Let $\Delta T=T-T_c$, with $a,c>0$ fixed. The equilibrium <phase diagram> has boundaries
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\Delta T&=0,&&b>0&&\text{continuous},\\
\Delta T&=\frac{3b^2}{16ac},&&b<0&&\text{first order}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
For either branch, larger $\Delta T$ is disordered and smaller $\Delta T$ is ordered. Both signs of the ordered <magnetization> are symmetry-related <global minima> throughout the ordered region, rather than separate regions selected by temperature and $b$.

The branches meet at the <tricritical point> $(\Delta T,b)=(0,0)$. Directly on $b=0$, minimizing $rm^2+cm^6/3$ gives
$$
\boxed{|m_{\rm eq}|=\left[\frac{a(T_c-T)}c\right]^{1/4}\quad(T<T_c),\qquad \beta_{\rm tri}=\frac14.}
$$
The transition there remains continuous, with a different <mean-field critical exponent>. For $b<0$, the metastability boundaries are $\Delta T=0$ for the disordered state and $\Delta T=b^2/(4ac)$ for the ordered state. The <first-order phase transition> curve lies strictly between them, as shown in the figure. These spinodal curves describe possible hysteretic continuation of local minima, not equilibrium phase boundaries.
